WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:09.466
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:16.308
Unit one: Rhetoric & Persuasion. Today you will analyse persuasive appeals using complex subordination.

4
00:00:16.608 --> 00:00:19.063
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.337
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:25.080
Ethos.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:30.149
Ethos means persuasion based on the speaker's credibility and character.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.829
The surgeon's ethos lent weight to her warning about smoking.

9
00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:36.709
Your turn. Say: Ethos.

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.475
Ethos.

11
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:42.218
Pathos.

12
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:46.795
Pathos means persuasion that appeals to the audience's emotions.

13
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:50.288
The charity's advert relied heavily on pathos.

14
00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:53.617
Your turn. Say: Pathos.

15
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:57.410
Pathos.

16
00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.116
Logos.

17
00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:03.591
Logos means persuasion through logic, evidence and reasoning.

18
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:07.890
His logos was impeccable, yet the audience remained unmoved.

19
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:10.834
Your turn. Say: Logos.

20
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.829
Logos.

21
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:16.735
Eloquence.

22
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.630
Eloquence means fluent, forceful and persuasive use of language.

23
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:25.273
Her eloquence silenced even her fiercest critics.

24
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:28.443
Your turn. Say: Eloquence.

25
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.406
Eloquence.

26
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.174
Oratory.

27
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:38.123
Oratory means the art of formal public speaking.

28
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:41.935
Churchill's wartime oratory is still studied today.

29
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.121
Your turn. Say: Oratory.

30
00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:49.165
Oratory.

31
00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:51.487
Rhetorical device.

32
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:56.735
Rhetorical device means a technique of language used to persuade or impress.

33
00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.998
Repetition is a rhetorical device that makes ideas memorable.

34
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:04.619
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical device.

35
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.071
Rhetorical device.

36
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:11.212
Persuasive appeal.

37
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:16.236
Persuasive appeal means a strategy designed to convince an audience.

38
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.289
Each persuasive appeal targets a different part of the listener.

39
00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:23.721
Your turn. Say: Persuasive appeal.

40
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:28.067
Persuasive appeal.

41
00:02:28.817 --> 00:02:30.480
Audience analysis.

42
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:36.118
Audience analysis means studying listeners' values and knowledge before speaking.

43
00:02:36.717 --> 00:02:40.214
Careful audience analysis shaped every example she chose.

44
00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:43.922
Your turn. Say: Audience analysis.

45
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.418
Audience analysis.

46
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:50.811
Discourse analysis.

47
00:02:51.817 --> 00:02:55.987
Discourse analysis means the study of how language works in real contexts.

48
00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:00.893
Discourse analysis revealed how politicians avoid direct answers.

49
00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:04.821
Your turn. Say: Discourse analysis.

50
00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:09.573
Discourse analysis.

51
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:11.515
Connotation.

52
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:17.070
Connotation means the feelings or ideas a word suggests beyond its literal meaning.

53
00:03:17.683 --> 00:03:21.227
'Thrifty' and 'stingy' share a meaning but differ in connotation.

54
00:03:21.817 --> 00:03:24.724
Your turn. Say: Connotation.

55
00:03:27.567 --> 00:03:28.579
Connotation.

56
00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:33.215
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:40.894
Visal, the minister's speech on road safety cited statistics for twenty minutes. Why did so few people remember it?

58
00:03:41.983 --> 00:03:51.207
Because logos alone rarely moves an audience. Although his evidence was sound, he never gave listeners a reason to care.

59
00:03:52.317 --> 00:03:54.862
So he should have told an emotional story instead?

60
00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:04.210
Not instead, but as well. Whereas pathos captures attention, logos sustains belief once the emotion has faded.

61
00:04:05.317 --> 00:04:07.107
And where does ethos fit in?

62
00:04:08.217 --> 00:04:19.379
It underpins both. Unless the audience trusts the speaker, neither stories nor statistics will persuade them, since credibility precedes every other appeal.

63
00:04:20.533 --> 00:04:22.521
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:23.383 --> 00:04:26.184
Grammar focus: Complex subordination.

65
00:04:26.484 --> 00:04:27.671
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:28.383 --> 00:04:32.333
Because logos appeals to reason, it often persuades skeptics.

67
00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.445
Although his evidence was sound, he failed to move the audience.

68
00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.812
Unless listeners trust the speaker, no argument will persuade them.

69
00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:57.190
C2 writers embed several subordinate clauses to show precise logical relations: 'Although the data were persuasive, the speech failed because, unless an audience trusts a speaker, evidence is ignored.' Vary subordinators rather than repeating 'because'.

70
00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:02.297
Be careful! Don't say: Despite he was credible, nobody listened.

71
00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:07.591
Say: Although he was credible, nobody listened.

72
00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:10.471
Despite + noun; although + clause.

73
00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:13.873
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:16.364
The Anatomy of Persuasion.

75
00:05:16.664 --> 00:05:23.761
Aristotle identified three persuasive appeals that remain central to rhetoric more than two thousand years after he described them.

76
00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:33.581
Ethos establishes the speaker's credibility, pathos engages the audience's emotions, and logos supplies the reasoning on which a conclusion rests.

77
00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:42.302
Although modern audiences claim to value evidence above all, research suggests that emotion frequently determines which evidence they are willing to accept.

78
00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:51.279
Skilled orators therefore conduct careful audience analysis, adjusting connotation, examples and tone to the values of their listeners.

79
00:05:52.267 --> 00:06:01.998
Because persuasion can serve manipulation as easily as truth, discourse analysis teaches citizens to recognise rhetorical devices rather than simply succumb to them.

80
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.287
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.009
blank the argument was logical, it failed to persuade.

82
00:06:11.309 --> 00:06:14.765
A, Because. B, Although. C, Unless.

83
00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.114
The answer is B: Although.

84
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.236
Persuasion based on the speaker's credibility is blank.

85
00:06:24.536 --> 00:06:28.271
A, ethos. B, pathos. C, logos.

86
00:06:31.833 --> 00:06:33.937
The answer is A: ethos.

87
00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:38.731
The feelings a word suggests beyond its literal meaning are its blank.

88
00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:42.929
A, oratory. B, discourse. C, connotation.

89
00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:48.838
The answer is C: connotation.

90
00:06:49.983 --> 00:06:52.018
Choose the most precise sentence.

91
00:06:52.318 --> 00:07:02.345
A, Unless trust exists, no evidence persuades.. B, Unless trust doesn't exist, evidence persuades.. C, Because unless trust, evidence persuades..

92
00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:10.465
The answer is A: Unless trust exists, no evidence persuades..

93
00:07:11.617 --> 00:07:15.393
According to the reading, discourse analysis teaches citizens to…

94
00:07:15.693 --> 00:07:21.610
A, avoid public speaking. B, recognise rhetorical devices. C, trust every speaker.

95
00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:28.513
The answer is B: recognise rhetorical devices.

96
00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.828
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:33.683 --> 00:07:37.239
Which rhetorical appeal do you find most persuasive, and why?

98
00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.597
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:55.897 --> 00:08:28.348
Personally, I find ethos the most persuasive, because I evaluate the messenger before I evaluate the message. Although a statistic may be accurate, I cannot judge its reliability unless I trust the person presenting it. Pathos certainly captures my attention, yet emotional stories alone can feel manipulative. When a credible doctor explains vaccine data calmly, all three appeals work together. Ultimately, credibility is the foundation on which both reason and emotion depend.

100
00:08:28.648 --> 00:08:30.392
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:31.283 --> 00:08:35.503
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and complex subordination.

102
00:08:35.803 --> 00:08:41.520
Today's value is eloquence. Choose your words with care, and speak to persuade honestly.

103
00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:44.229
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

104
00:08:44.529 --> 00:08:47.367
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

